---
<Gallery title="Calendar">
  <p class="demo-note">
    A month grid that says <strong>which days are special</strong> — public holidays, a
    plant shutdown, period-end, the dates a shipment cannot land on. It is
    <strong>not a date picker</strong>: entry stays on the native
    <code>&lt;input type="date"&gt;</code>, which is already localized, keyboard-accessible
    and native on a phone. Use this where the user needs to <em>see</em> the calendar.
  </p>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>One month, with the days that matter marked</h2>
    <Demo code={single} />
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">Weekends are hatched, the holiday carries a dot, today is
    outlined and the chosen day is filled. Every one of those is a shape as well as a
    colour, and each writes its meaning into the cell as visually-hidden text — so the
    grid reads the same in greyscale, under forced colours, and to a screen reader.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Three months — repetition, not a setting</h2>
    <Demo code={three} />
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">Put three <code>.bo-calendar__month</code> tables in one
    <code>.bo-calendar</code>. There is no <code>months</code> option to learn, and they
    wrap to one column when the space runs out, so the same markup serves a wide planning
    screen and a phone.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Why this exists, and why it is not a picker</h2>
    <p>The native date input is good and this framework keeps recommending it. What it
    cannot do is the whole reason for this component: <strong>its only constraints are
    <code>min</code>, <code>max</code> and <code>step</code></strong> — which describe a
    <em>range</em>. A works calendar is a <em>set</em>: eight public holidays, a two-week
    shutdown, the last working day of each period. There is no attribute for that, and no
    API to mark a day.</p>
    <p>So the split is deliberate: <strong>the browser owns picking a date, this owns
    showing which dates matter.</strong> Pair them — the native input for entry, this grid
    beside or below it for context — and you keep a control that is localized, works on a
    phone and needs no JavaScript from us.</p>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">It is a real <code>&lt;table&gt;</code> because a month is
    tabular data. That buys row and column semantics, <code>scope</code> headers and a
    caption for free: a screen reader announces "Tuesday, 12" without a line of ARIA.
    Each day carries a <code>&lt;time datetime&gt;</code>, so the machine-readable date is
    in the markup rather than inferred from the number.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Markup</h2>
    <pre><code>{single}</code></pre>
  </section>

  <ClassRef component="calendar" />
  <ApiTable component="calendar" notes={[
    'data-day is written by your app per render, like data-row-state — it is app state, not a variant.',
    'Every marked day needs its meaning in text; the colour and shape are the visible half only.',
    'A day may be a plain span, a link or a button. Only the last two get hover and pointer affordances.',
  ]} />
  <Related links={[
    ["/components/date", "Date value"],
    ["/components/form", "Form controls"],
    ["/patterns/filter-panel", "Advanced filter panel"],
    ["/concepts/accessibility", "Accessibility model"],
  ]} />
</Gallery>
